 \begin{abstract}
-The variational quantum eigensolver (VQE) combines quantum energy estimation with classical parameter optimization. Reliable execution requires correct control flow, numerical calculations, and reasoning about measurement uncertainty. We propose a four-layer verification architecture covering logical, numeric, probabilistic, and runtime properties. A shared execution model and specification library organize the requirements and their assumptions. A two-stage framework implements and evaluates the architecture: NuXmv, Dafny, and Storm establish properties before execution, and runtime verification checks the corresponding obligations in concrete executions. Probabilistic predictions are compared with repeated finite-shot executions. On a two-qubit benchmark, all \BaselineRuns executions satisfy the observed logical and numeric obligations within the verified scope. The Gaussian model and empirical executions show statistical agreement on bounded success and conditional resource use. Fault injection reveals complementary detection capabilities and limits in handling missing measurement records. Model growth and timing measurements quantify the cost of verification. These results demonstrate how heterogeneous verification methods can support a common assessment of VQE execution. Future work will strengthen the model-to-implementation correspondence and extend the framework to larger instances and hardware noise.
+The variational quantum eigensolver (VQE) combines quantum energy estimation with classical parameter optimization. Reliable execution requires correct control flow, numerical calculations, and reasoning about measurement uncertainty. We propose a four-layer verification architecture covering logical, numeric, probabilistic, and runtime properties. A shared execution model and specification library organize the requirements and their assumptions. A two-stage framework implements and evaluates the architecture: NuXmv, Dafny, and Storm establish properties before execution, and runtime verification checks the corresponding requirements in concrete executions. Probabilistic predictions are compared with repeated finite-shot executions. On a two-qubit benchmark, all \BaselineRuns executions satisfy the observed logical and numeric requirements within the verified scope. The Gaussian model and empirical executions show statistical agreement on bounded success and conditional resource use. Fault injection reveals complementary detection capabilities and limits in handling missing measurement records. Model growth and timing measurements quantify the cost of verification. These results demonstrate how heterogeneous verification methods can support a common assessment of VQE execution. Future work will strengthen the model-to-implementation correspondence and extend the framework to larger instances and hardware noise.
 \end{abstract}
 \begin{IEEEkeywords}
